.Language=English,English (English)
.PluginContents=RPN Calculator

@Contents
$ #RPN Calculator#
   RPN (Reverse Polish Notation) calculator for Far Manager.

   Operands are entered first, then the operator is applied to
   the topmost stack entries. For example, #3 4 + 5 *# leaves
   35 on the stack.

   ~Dialog and keys~@Dialog@
   ~Numbers and constants~@Numbers@
   ~Binary operators~@Operators@
   ~Functions~@Functions@
   ~Stack commands~@StackCmds@
   ~Number bases (bin, hex, dec)~@Bases@
   ~Angle mode~@Angles@
   ~Undo (undo, Ctrl+Backspace)~@Undo@
   ~Command line (rpn: prefix)~@CommandLine@
   ~Errors and persistence~@Errors@

@Dialog
$ #Dialog and keys#
   Open the calculator from the plugins menu (F11), item
   "RPN Calculator".

   The dialog shows the eight topmost stack levels. Level 1 (X)
   is at the bottom, next to the input line; level 2 is Y,
   level 3 is Z, and so on. Type commands separated by white
   space and press Enter. Several commands can be typed on one
   line.

   Keys in the input line:

   #Enter#                  Evaluate the line (empty line: DUP).
   #Ctrl+Enter#             Like Enter, then close the dialog and
                          insert X into the Far command line,
                          with all the digits it needs. On an empty
                          line it does not DUP: it only inserts X.
                          If the stack is empty (the line was
                          clr, for example) the dialog stays open
                          and the line is cleared, as with Enter.
                          If Far does not insert the text, a
                          message box shows X.
   #+  *  /  ^#             Empty line: apply the operator at once.
   #Backspace#              Empty line: DROP (remove X).
   #Del#                    Empty line: CLR (clear the whole stack).
                          (A line holding only blanks is not empty:
                          these keys then edit the line as usual.)
   #Ctrl+Backspace#         UNDO: restore the state before the
                          last command (see ~Undo~@Undo@).
   #Alt+F1#                 Subtract: Y - X (the - command).
   #Alt+F2#                 Change sign of X (chs).
   #Alt+F3#                 Reciprocal of X (inv).
   #Alt+F4#                 Swap X and Y (swap).
   #F1#                     This help.
   #Esc#                    Close the dialog. The stack is kept.

   Text already typed in the input line is evaluated before an
   Alt+F1...Alt+F4 command; if it contains an error, nothing is
   changed. If your terminal intercepts Alt+F4, type #swap#.

   Ctrl+Backspace works also when the input line contains text;
   the text is left as it is.

   ~Contents~@Contents@

@Numbers
$ #Numbers and constants#
   Decimal numbers: #3.14#, #-2#, #1e-5#. A decimal comma is
   accepted too: #3,14#.

   Binary, hexadecimal and decimal integers carry a suffix:
   1010##B is binary, 1F##H is hexadecimal and 255##D is a plain
   decimal integer (the form set by dec). Digits are 0-9 and
   A-F; the suffix and the digits are not case sensitive; a
   leading sign (+ or -) is allowed. The absolute value must
   not exceed 2^53 = 9007199254740992. See ~Number bases~@Bases@.

   A decimal number too large for a double is rejected (Invalid
   number). So is one that is not zero but too small to be
   represented: it would be read as 0. Subnormal values (down to
   about 5e-324) are accepted, and so are 0 and -0.

   Constants: #pi# and #e# push the constant on the stack.

   Command names are not case sensitive. Tokens are separated
   by white space (spaces or tabs).

   ~Contents~@Contents@

@Operators
$ #Binary operators#
   A binary operator takes Y and X and replaces them with the
   result. Example: #10 3 -# leaves 7.

   #+#  #-#  #*#  #/#             Y + X, Y - X, Y * X, Y / X.
   #^#  #**#                  Y raised to the power X.
   #%#  #mod#                 Remainder of Y / X (sign of Y).
   #atan2#                  Angle whose tangent is Y / X; both
                          signs select the quadrant. The result
                          is in the current ~angle mode~@Angles@.
   #min#  #max#               The smaller / the larger of Y and X.
                          For equal values, -0 is taken as
                          smaller than +0, whatever the order.

   Division by zero (/, %, mod) is an error.

   ~Contents~@Contents@

@Functions
$ #Functions#
   A function replaces X with its result.

   #neg#  #chs#               Change sign.
   #abs#                    Absolute value.
   #inv#                    Reciprocal, 1 / X (X must not be 0).
   #sq#                     X squared.
   #sqrt#                   Square root (X must not be negative).
   #cbrt#                   Cube root.
   #exp#                    e raised to the power X.
   #ln#                     Natural logarithm (X must be > 0).
   #log#                    Base 10 logarithm (X must be > 0).
   #log2#                   Base 2 logarithm (X must be > 0).
   #sin#  #cos#  #tan#          X is in the current ~angle mode~@Angles@.
                          A result smaller than four units in the
                          last place of the argument (about as
                          much as the argument is known to), like
                          cos(pi/2) or sin(pi), is replaced by
                          exactly 0, even if the function really
                          has a tiny value there. Not applied
                          when |X| is 1e9 or more. Small results
                          of small arguments are kept.
                          tan gives no error at its poles: for
                          example 90 deg tan gives a very large
                          number (about 1.6e16).
   #asin#  #acos#             X must be in -1..1. The result is in
                          the current angle mode.
   #atan#                   Arc tangent, in the current angle mode.
   #sinh#  #cosh#  #tanh#       Hyperbolic functions (not affected
                          by the angle mode).
   #fact#                   Factorial (X an integer, 0..170).
                          Exact up to 22!; beyond that, the
                          nearest double.
   #floor#                  Largest integer not greater than X.
   #ceil#                   Smallest integer not less than X.
   #round#                  Nearest integer (halves away from 0).
   #int#                    Integer part (truncation toward 0).

   ~Contents~@Contents@

@StackCmds
$ #Stack commands#
   #dup#                    Copy X to a new level.
   #drop#                   Remove X.
   #swap#                   Exchange X and Y.
   #over#                   Copy Y to the top (it becomes X).
   #rot#                    Rotate the three topmost levels:
                          Z becomes X, X becomes Y, Y becomes Z.
   #clr#  #clear#             Clear the whole stack.
   #undo#                   Undo the last command
                          (see ~Undo~@Undo@).
   #sum#                    Replace the whole stack with the sum
                          of all its entries.
   #avg#                    Replace the whole stack with the
                          average of all its entries.

   The stack can hold up to 10000 entries.

   ~Contents~@Contents@

@Bases
$ #Number bases (bin, hex, dec)#
   These commands change the way X is displayed. X must be an
   integer not greater than 2^53 in absolute value.

   #bin#                    Binary: 255 bin gives 11111111##B
   #hex#                    Hexadecimal: 255 hex gives FF##H
   #dec#                    Decimal: FF##H dec gives 255

   The value of X does not change, only the way it is shown.
   Negative numbers are shown with a minus sign: -10 bin gives
   -1010##B (this is not two's complement). dec shows X as a
   plain integer, never with an exponent.

   The forms can also be typed directly (1010##B, FF##H, 255##D)
   and are shown in that form until they are used in a
   calculation. The result of a calculation is shown in the
   normal form. dup, over, swap and rot keep the form.

   ~Contents~@Contents@

@Angles
$ #Angle mode#
   #rad#                    Angles in radians (the default).
   #deg#                    Angles in degrees.

   The mode affects sin, cos, tan, asin, acos, atan and atan2.
   The hyperbolic functions are not affected. The current mode
   is shown in the status line of the dialog and is saved
   together with the stack.

   ~Contents~@Contents@

@Undo
$ #Undo (undo, Ctrl+Backspace)#
   The #undo# command, or Ctrl+Backspace in the dialog, restores
   the stack and the angle mode to the state they had before the
   last command that changed them. Repeat it to go back further.

   Every command that changes something is one undo step,
   numbers included: after #3 4 +# three undos are needed to
   get back to an empty stack. A command that changes nothing
   (for example swap with two identical entries) is not recorded.

   #undo# can also be typed in the input line or after the
   rpn: prefix. It is not recorded itself, so there is no redo.

   Up to 100 steps are kept. The history is kept while Far is
   running, also between the dialog and the command line, but it
   is not saved in the Far settings. A line that contains an
   error changes neither the stack nor the history.

   If the history is empty, the error "Nothing to undo" is shown.

   ~Contents~@Contents@

@CommandLine
$ #Command line (rpn: prefix)#
   Type #rpn:# followed by the commands in the Far command line
   and press Enter, for example:

      rpn: 3 4 + 5 *

   The value of X and the stack depth are shown in a message
   box ("Stack is empty" when nothing is left). The stack and
   the angle mode are shared with the dialog and are kept
   between sessions.

   If the line contains an error, the error is reported and the
   stack is left as it was. If the result cannot be saved in the
   Far settings, the last line of the box says so (see
   ~Errors and persistence~@Errors@).

   To copy X from the dialog to the command line, press
   Ctrl+Enter.

   ~Contents~@Contents@

@Errors
$ #Errors and persistence#
   If a line contains an error, the whole line is cancelled:
   the stack and the angle mode return to their previous state.

   #Not enough operands#
      The command needs more entries than the stack holds.
   #Division by zero#
      Division, remainder or inv with a zero divisor.
   #Invalid result#
      The result is not a finite number: an overflow,
      or an undefined operation such as -8 0.5 ^.
   #Stack is full#
      The stack already holds 10000 entries.
   #Stack is empty#
      Ctrl+Enter was pressed but the line left the
      stack empty (for example clr).
   #Nothing to undo#
      The undo history is empty.
   #Unknown command: ...#
      The token is neither a number nor a command.
   #Invalid number: ...#
      The token is not a valid number, or it is too large for a
      double, or it is not zero but too small to be represented.
   #Invalid digit for the base: ...#
      A digit not allowed in the base, for example 12##B.
   #Number too large (max 2^53): ...#
      A number with a ##B, ##H or ##D suffix above 2^53.
   #Not an integer#
      bin, hex or dec was used with a fractional X.
   #Integer too large (max 2^53)#
      bin, hex or dec was used with X above 2^53.
   #Square root of a negative number#
   #Logarithm of a non-positive number#
   #Argument out of range (-1..1)#
      asin or acos with X outside -1..1.
   #Factorial needs an integer 0-170#
   #Cannot save the stack and the angle mode in the Far settings#
      Far did not accept the values, or the settings could not be
      opened, or the stored stack could not be read when the
      plugin was first used (it is then never overwritten). The
      calculator goes on working. The message is shown after the
      dialog is closed, or as the last line of the box of rpn:.
   #Cannot insert X into the command line#
      Far did not insert the text after Ctrl+Enter. The box shows
      the value of X, so that it is not lost.

   The stack and the angle mode are saved in the Far settings
   when the dialog is closed and after each successful rpn:
   command, together with the display forms set by bin, hex
   and dec. The undo history is not saved.

   If Far cannot open the settings when the plugin is first used,
   the calculator works on an empty stack and the stored one is
   not overwritten: nothing is saved until it has been read. The
   next use tries again.

   ~Contents~@Contents@
